(\"div\",{className:\"absolute l-0 r-0 b-0 h-[8px] rounded-l-lg rounded-r-lg\",style:{width:`${k(w?w:0)}%`,background:nT3(w?w:0,Z),printColorAdjust:\"exact\",WebkitPrintColorAdjust:\"exact\"}})}):null,B.map((J)=>{return Y(\"div\",{className:\"absolute b-0 h-[8px] bg-light-bg dark:bg-dark-bg w-[2px]\",style:{left:`${k(J)}%`,printColorAdjust:\"exact\",WebkitPrintColorAdjust:\"exact\"}},J)})]})},fM=rT3;var Do={NEO:{title:\"Revised NEO Personality Inventory\",description:`The Revised NEO Personality Inventory (NEO-PI-R) measures personality across 5 broad domains, each containing 6 underlying facets (30 facets in total). The NEO-PI-R is one of the most prominent assessments measuring the Big Five domains of personality—Neuroticism, Extraversion, Openness to Experience, Agreeableness, and Conscientiousness.\n      <br/><br/>\n      The NEO acronym reflects the test's original development as a 3-factor personality framework, with Agreeableness and Conscientious being added as part of a major revision in the 1980s. Several versions have been released since, along with dozens of translations and numerous public-domain alternatives. Collectively, these five-factor inventories are, by far, the most widely used assessments of personality in social science research. The public-domain AMBI version used here has a total of 108 questions, and was created using correlations between scores on the original NEO-PI-R and responses to a large pool of public-domain personality questions.`,scaleDisplayNames:{AMBI_NEO_Angry_hostility:\"Angry/Hostility\",AMBI_NEO_Self_consciousness:\"Self-Consciousness\",AMBI_NEO_Excitement_seeking:\"Excitement-Seeking\",AMBI_NEO_Tender_mindedness:\"Tender-Mindedness\",AMBI_NEO_Achievement_striving:\"Achievement-Striving\",AMBI_NEO_Self_discipline:\"Self-Discipline\",AMBI_NEO_Depression:\"Low Mood\",AMBI_NEO_Modesty:\"Low Attention-Seeking\"},scaleDescriptions:{AMBI_NEO_Neuroticism:\"Tendency to experience various negative emotions (like anxiety, sadness, and anger) as well as frequent or strong changes in emotions\",AMBI_NEO_Extraversion:\"Tendency to be sociable, talkative, assertive, and energetic\",AMBI_NEO_Openness_to_Experience:\"Tendency to pursue and enjoy new experiences, including unfamiliar topics, art (and other creative activities), and complex or intellectual pursuits\",AMBI_NEO_Agreeableness:\"Tendency to be affiliative, compassionate, sympathetic, and caring toward others\",AMBI_NEO_Conscientiousness:\"Tendency to be responsible, hard-working, organized, and achievement oriented\",AMBI_NEO_Anxiety:\"Tendency to feel fear over anticipated (or possible) future events\",AMBI_NEO_Angry_hostility:\"Tendency to have strong negative reactions to perceived harm or threat\",AMBI_NEO_Depression:\"Tendency to feel sadness, negative mood, and/or low interest in activities\",AMBI_NEO_Self_consciousness:\"Tendency to be shy or embarrassed about one’s behavior or appearance\",AMBI_NEO_Impulsiveness:\"Tendency to act without thinking of the consequences\",AMBI_NEO_Vulnerability:\"Tendency to lose composure when faced with stressful circumstances\",AMBI_NEO_Warmth:\"Tendency to make connections with others by showing interest and kindness\",AMBI_NEO_Gregariousness:\"Tendency to prefer (and seek out) stimulating social activities\",AMBI_NEO_Assertiveness:\"Tendency to take charge and to make one’s opinions known\",AMBI_NEO_Activity:\"Tendency to have a lot of energy and to prefer an active lifestyle\",AMBI_NEO_Excitement_seeking:\"Tendency to prefer stimulating activities\",AMBI_NEO_Positive_emotions:\"Tendency to be happy and enthusiastic\",AMBI_NEO_Fantasy:\"Tendency to have an active imagination and capacity for creativity\",AMBI_NEO_Aesthetics:\"Tendency to appreciate art (of various types) and natural beauty\",AMBI_NEO_Feelings:\"Capacity to understand and recognize emotions (in oneself and others)\",AMBI_NEO_Actions:\"Tendency to prefer new experiences over familiar ones\",AMBI_NEO_Ideas:\"Tendency to be intellectually curious\",AMBI_NEO_Values:\"Tendency to be liberal and tolerant\",AMBI_NEO_Trust:\"Tendency to trust others and expect good intentions\",AMBI_NEO_Straightforwardness:\"Tendency to be honest and act with transparency\",AMBI_NEO_Altruism:\"Tendency to be helpful, selfless, and generous\",AMBI_NEO_Compliance:\"Tendency to be cooperative\",AMBI_NEO_Modesty:\"Tendency to avoid attention and not make one’s strengths known to others\",AMBI_NEO_Tender_mindedness:\"Tendency to be compassionate and empathetic\",AMBI_NEO_Competence:\"Capacity to handle tasks successfully\",AMBI_NEO_Order:\"Tendency to stay organized\",AMBI_NEO_Dutifulness:\"Tendency to meet one’s obligations\",AMBI_NEO_Achievement_striving:\"Tendency to be ambitiously goal oriented\",AMBI_NEO_Self_discipline:\"Capacity to resist temptations for the sake of long-term goals\",AMBI_NEO_Deliberation:\"Tendency to use careful judgment and to plan before proceeding\"}},HEXACO:{title:\"HEXACO Personality Inventory\",description:`The HEXACO Personality Inventory is among the most prominent 6-factor alternatives to the more famous Big Five personality models. In practice, the HEXACO and the Big Five have a lot in common. Both have highly similar dimensions in Extraversion, Conscientiousness, and Openness. But, instead of Neuroticism in the Big Five, the HEXACO has Emotionality which contains some similar content but less focus on emotional stability. The biggest difference between the two models is that the HEXACO contains an additional dimension labeled Honesty-Humility, and this inclusion also affects the ways that each model measures Agreeableness. Big Five Agreeableness includes facets of Trust and Straightforwardness, but these are part of Honesty-Humility in the HEXACO. HEXACO Agreeableness is more focused on patience and forgiveness than Big Five Agreeableness.\n    <br/><br/>\n    The HEXACO (Honesty/Humility, Emotionality, eXtraversion, Agreeableness, Conscientiousness, and Openness) is also newer than the Big Five, having been first introduced in the early 2000s. The public-domain AMBI version used here has a total of 88 questions, and was created using correlations between scores on the original HEXACO and responses to a large pool of public-domain personality questions.`,scaleDisplayNames:{AMBI_HEXACO_Honesty_Humility:\"Honesty-Humility\",AMBI_HEXACO_Aesthetic_appreciation:\"Art Appreciation\",AMBI_HEXACO_Agreeableness:\"Agreeable Emotions\",AMBI_HEXACO_Anxiety:\"Worry\",AMBI_HEXACO_Extraversion:\"Socially Extraverted\",AMBI_HEXACO_Openness_to_Experience:\"Intellectual Openness\"},scaleDescriptions:{AMBI_HEXACO_Honesty_Humility:\"Tendency to be fair, open, and humble in interpersonal interactions\",AMBI_HEXACO_Emotionality:\"Tendency to experience various negative emotions, especially worry, fear, and depressed mood\",AMBI_HEXACO_Extraversion:\"Tendency to be lively, sociable, and active, typically associated with higher levels of enthusiasm and cheerfulness\",AMBI_HEXACO_Agreeableness:\"Tendency to be compassionate and caring, patient with others, and forgiving\",AMBI_HEXACO_Conscientiousness:\"Tendency to be diligent, well-organized, and interested in doing tasks promptly and well\",AMBI_HEXACO_Openness_to_Experience:\"Tendency to be unconventional, intellectual, curious, and interested in creative pursuits\",AMBI_HEXACO_Sincerity:\"Tendency to be genuine and straightforward\",AMBI_HEXACO_Fairness:\"Tendency to be open and equitable when dealing with others\",AMBI_HEXACO_Greed_avoidance:\"Tendency to avoid prioritizing one’s own (material) interests\",AMBI_HEXACO_Modesty:\"Tendency to be humble instead of boastful or attention-seeking\",AMBI_HEXACO_Fearfulness:\"Tendency to find stressful or challenging situations frightening\",AMBI_HEXACO_Anxiety:\"Tendency to worry about things (especially future events)\",AMBI_HEXACO_Dependence:\"Tendency to be reliant on others and to need frequent reassurance\",AMBI_HEXACO_Sentimentality:\"Tendency to be emotionally sensitive and empathetic\",AMBI_HEXACO_Expressiveness:\"Tendency to be talkative, especially about emotions and personal experiences\",AMBI_HEXACO_Social_boldness:\"Tendency to be assertive and confident in social interactions\",AMBI_HEXACO_Sociability:\"Tendency to enjoy the company of others (including large group activities)\",AMBI_HEXACO_Liveliness:\"Tendency to be energetic and enthusiastic\",AMBI_HEXACO_Forgiveness:\"Tendency to forgive and maintain a positive view of others\",AMBI_HEXACO_Gentleness:\"Tendency to accept people and avoid being judgmental of others\",AMBI_HEXACO_Flexibility:\"Capacity to be adaptable and take input from others\",AMBI_HEXACO_Patience:\"Tendency to remain calm and avoid feeling irritated\",AMBI_HEXACO_Organization:\"Tendency to keep things tidy and orderly\",AMBI_HEXACO_Diligence:\"Tendency to be industrious and goal oriented\",AMBI_HEXACO_Perfectionism:\"Desire for precision and thoroughness\",AMBI_HEXACO_Prudence:\"Tendency to plan ahead and proceed carefully\",AMBI_HEXACO_Aesthetic_appreciation:\"Tendency to enjoy (and engage with) nature and the arts\",AMBI_HEXACO_Inquisitiveness:\"Tendency to be curious\",AMBI_HEXACO_Creativity:\"Tendency to imagine, create, and be innovative\",AMBI_HEXACO_Unconventionality:\"Willingness to resist norms and be eccentric\"}},MPQ:{title:\"Multidimensional Personality Questionnaire\",description:`The Multidimensional Personality Questionnaire assesses 3 broad dimensions of temperament—Positive Emotionality, Negative Emotionality, and Constraint. Beneath these, the MPQ also measures 11 more narrow traits, though one of these 11 (Absorption) lies outside of the 3 higher dimensions. \n      <br/><br/>\n      Importantly, the MPQ was created after many years of informal exploratory research into the dimensions of personality that seemed to show up repeatedly across personality and clinical psychology research projects. In this way, it has more in common with the JPI-R than the NEO and HEXACO models. That said, the MPQ is unique (among frameworks in the AMBI) in being more closely aligned with clinical psychology research topics—this may be due to 2 of the 3 broad factors being rooted in emotionality.\n      <br/><br/>\n      The public-domain AMBI version used here has a total of 42 questions, and was created using correlations between scores on the original MPQ and responses to a large pool of public-domain personality questions.\n    `,scaleDisplayNames:{AMBI_MPQ_Well_being:\"Well-Being\"},scaleDescriptions:{AMBI_MPQ_Positive_Emotionality:\"A tendency to engage positively with rewarding stimuli, especially people and ideas. High positive emotionality is associated with more joy and enthusiasm and lower levels with low mood and low energy levels\",AMBI_MPQ_Negative_Emotionality:\"A tendency to experience strong negative mood states like stress, anxiety, and anger. Low negative emotionality is associated with emotional stability and low aggression\",AMBI_MPQ_Constraint:\"A tendency for more controlled behavior, meaning less impulsivity and risk-seeking behavior and higher conformity and conventionality\",AMBI_MPQ_Well_being:\"Tendency to feel happy, satisfied with one’s life, and optimistic about the future\",AMBI_MPQ_Social_potency:\"Capacity for influencing and leading others\",AMBI_MPQ_Achievement:\"Tendency to be ambitious, hard-working, and goal-oriented\",AMBI_MPQ_Social_closeness:\"Interest in developing and maintaining social relationships\",AMBI_MPQ_Stress_reaction:\"Tendency to be reactive to stress, worry, and feel anxious\",AMBI_MPQ_Aggression:\"Tendency to initiate conflicts, intimidate, and be confrontational\",AMBI_MPQ_Alienation:\"Tendency to feel taken advantage of by others, expecting negative outcomes\",AMBI_MPQ_Control:\"Tendency for low impulsivity, more planning, and more self-control\",AMBI_MPQ_Harm_avoidance:\"Tendency and preference for avoiding dangerous activities\",AMBI_MPQ_Traditionalism:\"Tendency to hold more traditional and conservative worldviews\",AMBI_MPQ_Absorption:\"Tendency to become deeply involved in cognitive and/or sensory experiences\"}},CPI:{title:\"California Personality Inventory\",description:`The California Personality Inventory (CPI) is the oldest personality framework among those captured by the AMBI, having been first introduced in 1956. It is related to a popular measure of clinical topics called the MMPI in that it was developed using a similar strategy of aggregating many individual questions that were expected to tap into enduring aspects of personality rather than being based on personality theory or the lexical structure of personality descriptions. Like the MMPI, the original CPI is also a very long assessment, with more than 400 questions.\n      <br/><br/>\n    From these many questions, many scores can be produced, including 20 primary scales plus several others (9 more are included in the AMBI). The primary 20 scales of the CPI are sometimes aggregated into 4 broad categories, though these are too diverse to be converted into scored categories. They are Interpersonal Characteristics (which includes the scales for Dominance, Capacity for Status, Sociability, Social Presence, Self Acceptance, Independence, and Empathy), Self-Management Characteristics (Responsibility, Socialization, Self Control, Good Impression, Communality, Well Being, Tolerance), Motivational Style (Achievement via Conformance, Achievement via Independence, Intellectual Efficiency), and Cognitive Style (Psychological Mindedness, Flexibility, Sensitivity). The public-domain AMBI version used here has a total of 55 questions and was created using correlations between scores on the original version and responses to a large pool of public personality questions.`,scaleDisplayNames:{AMBI_CPI_Self_control:\"Self-Control\",AMBI_CPI_Norm_doubting_vs_Norm_favoring:\"Norm-Doubting vs Norm-Favoring\",AMBI_CPI_Tough_mindedness:\"Tough-Mindedness\",AMBI_CPI_Achievement_via_conformance:\"Valuing traditional achievements\",AMBI_CPI_Achievement_via_independence:\"Valuing unique achievements\",AMBI_CPI_Dominance:\"Social Dominance\",AMBI_CPI_Empathy:\"Social Fluency\",AMBI_CPI_Flexibility:\"Tolerance for Ambiguity\",AMBI_CPI_Good_impression:\"Personable\",AMBI_CPI_Independence:\"Self-Reliance\",AMBI_CPI_Responsibility:\"Compliance\",AMBI_CPI_Sociability:\"Social Skill\",AMBI_CPI_Tolerance:\"Composure\",AMBI_CPI_Well_being:\"Low Vulnerability\"},scaleDescriptions:{AMBI_CPI_Interpersonal_characteristics:\"The constellation of trait scores relating to the ways that one interacts with others. This includes preferences for working alone or with others, the tendency to be socially confident, assertive, and affiliative\",AMBI_CPI_Self_management_characteristics:\"The constellation of traits relating to self-regulatory behaviors, including those relevant to both social and personal concerns\",AMBI_CPI_Motivational_style:\"The constellation of traits relating to preferences for goal pursuit, including the extent to which one conforms to rules and guidelines, thinks independently, and works efficiently at a high level\",AMBI_CPI_Cognitive_style:\"Tendency to be aware of (and influenced by) psychological factors affecting oneself and close others\",AMBI_CPI_Dominance:\"Tendency to take charge and be assertive\",AMBI_CPI_Capacity_for_status:\"Tendency to think and engage in higher-level discussions and tasks\",AMBI_CPI_Sociability:\"Tendency to seek out social interactions and be comfortable with them\",AMBI_CPI_Social_presence:\"Tendency to be charismatic, personable, and socially adept\",AMBI_CPI_Self_acceptance:\"Tendency to be willing to take chances, make mistakes, and play with new ideas\",AMBI_CPI_Independence:\"Capacity to be self-reliant, especially in dealing with complex problems\",AMBI_CPI_Empathy:\"Tendency to be interested in and concerned about other people’s circumstances\",AMBI_CPI_Responsibility:\"Tendency to be conscientious, cooperative, and respectful of others\",AMBI_CPI_Socialization:\"Tendency to be socially trusting, collaborative, and fair\",AMBI_CPI_Self_control:\"Capacity to remain emotionally regulated and resist rash or impulsive reactions\",AMBI_CPI_Good_impression:\"Tendency to be even-tempered (resisting temptations and impropriety)\",AMBI_CPI_Communality:\"Tendency to conform to socially accepted patterns of behavior and to be conventional\",AMBI_CPI_Well_being:\"Tendency to feel satisfied with one’s circumstances and optimistic about the future\",AMBI_CPI_Tolerance:\"Tendency to be open-minded, compassionate, and sympathetic\",AMBI_CPI_Achievement_via_conformance:\"Tendency to pursue success by adhering to rules and guidelines\",AMBI_CPI_Achievement_via_independence:\"Tendency to pursue success by exploring new or unconventional paths\",AMBI_CPI_Intellectual_efficiency:\"Tendency to learn or use advanced skills, be well-informed, and behave in an educated manner\",AMBI_CPI_Psychological_mindedness:\"Tendency to have (or seek) insight into others’ behavior\",AMBI_CPI_Flexibility:\"Tendency to be adaptable and open to adopting new ways of thinking\",AMBI_CPI_Sensitivity:\"Tendency to be sympathetic and emotional about the difficulties of others\",AMBI_CPI_Externality_vs_Internality:\"Tendency to seek or avoid attention\",AMBI_CPI_Norm_doubting_vs_Norm_favoring:\"Tendency to conform (or not) with social norms and moral standards without questioning\",AMBI_CPI_Realization:\"Extent to which one is embracing (living consistently with) their profile in terms of externality/internality and norm-doubting vs norm-favoring\",AMBI_CPI_Managerial_potential:\"Potential (capacity/interest) for performing well in a managerial role\",AMBI_CPI_Work_orientation:\"Potential (capacity/interest) to work productively and manage challenges with resilience\",AMBI_CPI_Creative_temperament:\"Tendency to be interested in creative and innovative pursuits\",AMBI_CPI_Leadership:\"Capacity to lead others effectively and productively\",AMBI_CPI_Amicability:\"Tendency to be easy to get along with, understanding, and friendly\",AMBI_CPI_Tough_mindedness:\"Tendency to be poised, objective, and resilient\"}},JPIR:{title:\"Jackson Personality Inventory – Revised\",description:`The Jackson Personality Inventory was originally designed as a comprehensive measure of 15 important topics in personality and social psychology in the 1970s. While most of these continue to be researched frequently today, they often use different labels or framings. A revision of the JPI in the mid-1990s renamed 6 of the original scales and several subsequent research efforts have tried to organize these scales into the Big Five dimensions. Since these results have been inconsistent, no higher-level grouping of the scales is provided here.\n    <br/><br/>\n    An important finding from research using the JPI-R has been evidence that several scales are not well (or consistently) captured by personality frameworks based on the Big Five. This evidence has contributed to increasing recognition that the Big Five is less comprehensive and generalizable than originally believed. The public-domain AMBI version of the JPI-R used here has a total of 66 questions, and was created using correlations between scores on the original JPI-R and responses to a large pool of public-domain personality questions.`,scaleDisplayNames:{AMBI_JPIR_Risk_taking:\"Risk-Taking\",AMBI_JPIR_Sociability:\"Connection\"},scaleDescriptions:{AMBI_JPIR_Complexity:\"Tendency to prefer intellectually stimulating topics\",AMBI_JPIR_Breadth_of_interest:\"Preference for a wide variety of cognitive interests and activities\",AMBI_JPIR_Innovation:\"Tendency to come up with new and creative ideas\",AMBI_JPIR_Tolerance:\"Tendency to be open to other ways of thinking and doing things\",AMBI_JPIR_Sociability:\"Tendency to enjoy the company of others\",AMBI_JPIR_Social_confidence:\"Capacity to be comfortable interacting with others\",AMBI_JPIR_Energy_level:\"Tendency to be highly active, always doing things\",AMBI_JPIR_Empathy:\"Tendency to share/feel the emotions of others\",AMBI_JPIR_Anxiety:\"Tendency to worry and become stressed easily\",AMBI_JPIR_Cooperativeness:\"Tendency to go along with the ideas and opinions of others\",AMBI_JPIR_Social_astuteness:\"Capacity to influence others (sometimes for one’s own benefit)\",AMBI_JPIR_Risk_taking:\"Tendency to be comfortable with activities that others consider dangerous\",AMBI_JPIR_Organization:\"Tendency to prefer (and maintain) order\",AMBI_JPIR_Traditional_values:\"Tendency to hold conservative worldviews\",AMBI_JPIR_Responsibility:\"Tendency to fulfill one’s obligations\"}},\"6FPQ\":{title:\"Six Factor Personality Questionnaire\",description:`The Six-Factor Personality Questionnaire (6FPQ) was developed in the late 1990s as an extension and revision of an older framework called the Personality Research Form (PRF), which aimed to assess the 20 “manifest needs” described in a classic book about personality from the 1930s (Murray, 1938). \n    <br/><br/>\n    Motivated by the growing popularity of the Big Five, the creator of the PRF (Douglas Jackson, who also developed the JPI-R) re-analyzed and reorganized its scales to produce a more simple six-factor framework—the 6FPQ. He labeled these dimensions Extraversion, Agreeableness, Independence, Openness to Experience, Methodicalness, and Industriousness. Each has 3 underlying scales. The public-domain AMBI version used here has a total of 69 questions, and was created using correlations between scores on the original 6FPQ and responses to a large pool of public-domain personality questions.`,scaleDisplayNames:{AMBI_6FPQ_Good_natured:\"Good-Natured\",AMBI_6FPQ_Self_reliance:\"Self-Reliance\",AMBI_6FPQ_Abasement:\"Deference\",AMBI_6FPQ_Achievement:\"Goal-orientation\",AMBI_6FPQ_Agreeableness:\"Kindness\",AMBI_6FPQ_Breadth_of_interest:\"Curiosity\",AMBI_6FPQ_Deliberativeness:\"Planfulness\",AMBI_6FPQ_Even_tempered:\"Mild-Mannered\",AMBI_6FPQ_Extraversion:\"Assertive Social Presence\"},scaleDescriptions:{AMBI_6FPQ_Extraversion:\"Tendency to be outgoing, talkative, comfortable in social interactions, and assertive\",AMBI_6FPQ_Agreeableness:\"Tendency to be forgiving, tolerant, kind, and compassionate\",AMBI_6FPQ_Methodicalness:\"Tendency to be careful, planful, and orderly\",AMBI_6FPQ_Independence:\"Tendency to be less concerned with the approval and concern of others\",AMBI_6FPQ_Openness_to_Experience:\"Tendency to be willing to engage with new topics, especially new ideas, and to engage with complex and abstract concepts\",AMBI_6FPQ_Industriousness:\"Tendency to have a strong work ethic, to be industrious, and stay busy and productive\",AMBI_6FPQ_Affiliation:\"Tendency to seek community with others by building and maintaining close personal relationships\",AMBI_6FPQ_Dominance:\"Tendency to prefer having authority over others, whether as part of a leadership role or in a more manipulative, self-interested manner\",AMBI_6FPQ_Exhibition:\"Tendency to be attention-seeking, including a desire to show off and entertain others\",AMBI_6FPQ_Abasement:\"Tendency to be submissive, often associated with lower self-esteem and a relatively higher tolerance for being taken advantage of\",AMBI_6FPQ_Even_tempered:\"Tendency to have a stable demeanor (rarely becoming irritable or upset)\",AMBI_6FPQ_Good_natured:\"Tendency to be cooperative, tolerant, and open to feedback from others\",AMBI_6FPQ_Cognitive_structure:\"Tendency to prefer traditional values and authoritarian worldviews\",AMBI_6FPQ_Deliberativeness:\"Tendency to resist impulsive and thoughtless behavior\",AMBI_6FPQ_Order:\"Tendency to prefer organization, tidiness, as well as to carry out activities in a planful, methodical manner\",AMBI_6FPQ_Autonomy:\"Tendency to prefer doing things alone, including working independently\",AMBI_6FPQ_Individualism:\"Tendency to be unconcerned by the opinions and preferences of others\",AMBI_6FPQ_Self_reliance:\"Tendency to get things done for oneself, without needing (or bothering) to consult with others\",AMBI_6FPQ_Change:\"Tendency to be adaptable and open to new ways of doing things rather than highly habitual\",AMBI_6FPQ_Understanding:\"Tendency to be intellectual and motivated to learn\",AMBI_6FPQ_Breadth_of_interest:\"Tendency to prefer having a wide variety of pursuits (hobbies and interests) including those that are new and unfamiliar\",AMBI_6FPQ_Achievement:\"Tendency to prioritize productivity, dedication to one’s goals, and to go above and beyond the expectations of others\",AMBI_6FPQ_Endurance:\"Tendency to be resilient and perseverant, even when dealing with complex challenges\",AMBI_6FPQ_Seriousness:\"Tendency to avoid opportunities for creativity and playfulness, including an avoidance of spontaneity and silly fun\"}},TCI:{title:\"Temperament & Character Inventory\",description:`The Temperament & Character Inventory (TCI) measures personality across 7 broad dimensions, and each of these contains 3-4 lower dimensions. The broad dimensions are loosely considered to represent “temperament” (the dimensions of Novelty Seeking, Harm Avoidance, Reward Dependence and Persistence), as well as “character” (Self Directedness, Cooperativeness, and Self Transcendence).\n    <br/><br/>\n    The original version of the TCI was developed in the early 1990s by Robert Cloninger, and was based on an earlier assessment known as the Tridimensional Personality Questionnaire. Multiple versions have been published since the original, including translations of the TCI into more than a dozen languages. The public-domain AMBI version used here has a total of 100 questions and was created using correlations between scores on the original English version and responses to a large pool of public personality questions.`,scaleDisplayNames:{AMBI_TCI_Novelty_Seeking:\"Novelty-Seeking\",AMBI_TCI_Self_Directedness:\"Self-Directedness\",AMBI_TCI_Cooperativeness:\"Cooperativeness\",AMBI_TCI_Self_Transcendence:\"Self-Transcendence\",AMBI_TCI_Work_hardened:\"Work-Hardened\",AMBI_TCI_Self_acceptance:\"Self-Acceptance\",AMBI_TCI_Self_forgetful:\"Self-Forgetful\",AMBI_TCI_Dependence:\"Conformity\",AMBI_TCI_Eagerness_of_effort:\"Initiative\",AMBI_TCI_Enlightened_second_nature:\"Restraint\",AMBI_TCI_Exploratory_excitability:\"Sensation-Seeking\",AMBI_TCI_Pure_hearted_conscience:\"Morality\",AMBI_TCI_Responsibility:\"Resilience\",AMBI_TCI_Reward_Dependence:\"Sensitivity to Rewards\",AMBI_TCI_Transpersonal_identification:\"Sensitive to Beauty\",AMBI_TCI_Warmth:\"Social Engagement\"},scaleDescriptions:{AMBI_TCI_Novelty_Seeking:\"Tendency to pursue new experiences, especially those that are emotionally or cognitively stimulating\",AMBI_TCI_Harm_Avoidance:\"A tendency to avoid risk and distress (physical, social, and emotional)\",AMBI_TCI_Reward_Dependence:\"Tendency to be highly sensitive to rewards, including intangible social and emotional reward cues\",AMBI_TCI_Persistence:\"Tendency to be productive, goal-oriented, and resilient when managing challenges\",AMBI_TCI_Self_Directedness:\"Capacity to adapt oneself to situational demands—a character trait that typically requires well-developed psychological skills\",AMBI_TCI_Cooperativeness:\"Capacity to collaborate and work well with others, a dimension that is typically associated with traits like empathy, compassion, and tolerance\",AMBI_TCI_Self_Transcendence:\"Capacity to see oneself as part of something that extends beyond one’s own personal experiences of the world\",AMBI_TCI_Exploratory_excitability:\"Tendency to seek out and enjoy new and thrilling experiences\",AMBI_TCI_Impulsiveness:\"Tendency to act quickly without considering the consequences\",AMBI_TCI_Extravagance:\"Tendency to spend money freely and prioritize immediate enjoyment\",AMBI_TCI_Disorderliness:\"Tendency to prefer flexibility and spontaneity over adherence to rules and order\",AMBI_TCI_Worry_and_pessimism:\"Tendency to frequently and excessively worry about potential future problems\",AMBI_TCI_Fear_of_uncertainty:\"Tendency to feel anxious in situations where most others perceive little risk\",AMBI_TCI_Shyness:\"Tendency to feel discomfort or lack of confidence in social situations (especially with strangers)\",AMBI_TCI_Fatigability:\"Tendency to get fatigued more quickly and recover more slowly than most others\",AMBI_TCI_Sentimentality:\"Tendency to be guided by emotions and experience nostalgia about past events\",AMBI_TCI_Warmth:\"Tendency to be genuinely interested in people and to enjoy social interactions\",AMBI_TCI_Attachment:\"Tendency to be open about one’s feelings and interested in close relationships\",AMBI_TCI_Dependence:\"Tendency to seek approval, support, and direction from others\",AMBI_TCI_Eagerness_of_effort:\"Tendency to be enthusiastic about starting and completing projects\",AMBI_TCI_Work_hardened:\"Tendency to persistently and competently handle challenging tasks\",AMBI_TCI_Ambitious:\"Tendency to be goal oriented and industrious\",AMBI_TCI_Perfectionist:\"Tendency to set and pursue high standards, often at the expense of more immediate rewards\",AMBI_TCI_Responsibility:\"Capacity to stay focused on duties and obligations, especially those affecting others\",AMBI_TCI_Purposefulness:\"Capacity to maintain a strong sense of purpose and meaning in life\",AMBI_TCI_Resourcefulness:\"Capacity to draw on a wide range of skills rather than give up on hard problems\",AMBI_TCI_Self_acceptance:\"Capacity to maintain a healthy and realistic view of oneself\",AMBI_TCI_Enlightened_second_nature:\"Capacity for self-control (resisting temptations and bad habits)\",AMBI_TCI_Social_acceptance:\"Capacity to remain open and respectful of multiple diverse perspectives\",AMBI_TCI_Empathy:\"Capacity to feel and understand the emotions of others\",AMBI_TCI_Helpfulness:\"Capacity to enjoy helping others and to value their contributions\",AMBI_TCI_Compassion:\"Capacity to accept others and to forgive their mistakes\",AMBI_TCI_Pure_hearted_conscience:\"Capacity to adhere to moral standards\",AMBI_TCI_Self_forgetful:\"Tendency to become absorbed in thoughts and experiences\",AMBI_TCI_Transpersonal_identification:\"Capacity to appreciate beauty and profound experiences\",AMBI_TCI_Spiritual_acceptance:\"Tendency to have spiritual experiences or to feel supernatural connections\"}},HPI:{title:\"Hogan Personality Inventory\",description:`The Hogan Personality Inventory was created in the 1980s and is one of the most well-validated personality frameworks available, mainly due to the fact that it has been updated frequently. The AMBI version used here can estimate HPI scores for 44 low-level scales, but other versions of the HPI provide a range of higher level scores, including ones similar to the Big Five and beyond. The HPI can also be used to predict certain aspects of job performance, like the potential for success in various job roles (managerial, clerical, etc) and work environments.\n    <br/><br/>\n    The public-domain AMBI version used here generates estimates for the 44 lower level scales only (using 121 questions), and was created using correlations between scores on an older version of the HPI and responses to a large pool of public personality questions.`,scaleDisplayNames:{AMBI_HPI_Even_tempered:\"Even-Tempered\",AMBI_HPI_Self_confidence:\"Self-Confidence\",AMBI_HPI_Experience_seeking:\"Experience-Seeking\",AMBI_HPI_Thrill_seeking:\"Thrill-Seeking\",AMBI_HPI_Self_focus:\"Self-Focus\",AMBI_HPI_Avoids_trouble:\"Avoiding Trouble\",AMBI_HPI_Easy_to_live_with:\"Affable\",AMBI_HPI_Empathy:\"Low Irritability\",AMBI_HPI_Good_attachment:\"Secure Attachment\",AMBI_HPI_Leadership:\"Self-Directed\",AMBI_HPI_Likes_crowds:\"Liking Crowds\",AMBI_HPI_Likes_parties:\"Liking Parties\",AMBI_HPI_Likes_people:\"Liking People\",AMBI_HPI_No_depression:\"Stability\",AMBI_HPI_No_guilt:\"Self-Assured\",AMBI_HPI_No_hostility:\"Forgiving\",AMBI_HPI_No_social_anxiety:\"Social Self-Esteem\",AMBI_HPI_No_somatic_complaints:\"Vitality\",AMBI_HPI_Not_anxious:\"Steadiness\",AMBI_HPI_Not_autonomous:\"Approval Seeking\",AMBI_HPI_Not_spontaneous:\"Grounded\"},scaleDescriptions:{AMBI_HPI_Empathy:\"Capacity to understand others’ feelings and respond with compassion\",AMBI_HPI_Not_anxious:\"Tendency to remain calm and worry-free\",AMBI_HPI_No_guilt:\"Tendency to be comfortable with oneself and to have little regret for previous actions\",AMBI_HPI_Calmness:\"Tendency to remain calm and composed (especially under pressure)\",AMBI_HPI_Even_tempered:\"Tendency to rarely get upset\",AMBI_HPI_No_somatic_complaints:\"Tendency to experience few physical symptoms (especially ones related to stress)\",AMBI_HPI_Trusting:\"Tendency to trust other people and their intentions\",AMBI_HPI_Good_attachment:\"Tendency to expect stability in relationships\",AMBI_HPI_Competitive:\"Tendency to be willing to accept challenges and to expect success\",AMBI_HPI_Self_confidence:\"Tendency to maintain positive self-regard and assuredness\",AMBI_HPI_No_depression:\"Capacity to avoid experiencing low mood and disinterest\",AMBI_HPI_Leadership:\"Tendency to be willing to take charge and influence or direct others\",AMBI_HPI_Identity:\"Capacity for self-awareness and a sense of direction in life\",AMBI_HPI_No_social_anxiety:\"Tendency to be comfortable with and skilled at social situations\",AMBI_HPI_Likes_parties:\"Tendency to enjoy social gatherings\",AMBI_HPI_Likes_crowds:\"Tendency to enjoy large public events (sporting events, concerts)\",AMBI_HPI_Experience_seeking:\"Tendency to enjoy trying new things\",AMBI_HPI_Exhibitionistic:\"Tendency to enjoy attention from others\",AMBI_HPI_Entertaining:\"Tendency to enjoy amusing others\",AMBI_HPI_Easy_to_live_with:\"Tendency to be accepting of others and easy going\",AMBI_HPI_Sensitive:\"Tendency to feel strong emotions about the experiences of others\",AMBI_HPI_Caring:\"Tendency to feel concern for others\",AMBI_HPI_Likes_people:\"Tendency to be interested in the experiences of others\",AMBI_HPI_No_hostility:\"Tendency to forgive easily and forget disagreements\",AMBI_HPI_Moralistic:\"Tendency to be judgmental of the behavior of others\",AMBI_HPI_Mastery:\"Tendency to persevere until expert performance is routinely achieved\",AMBI_HPI_Virtuous:\"Tendency to behave with high moral standards and goodness\",AMBI_HPI_Not_autonomous:\"Tendency to be dependent on others and approval seeking\",AMBI_HPI_Not_spontaneous:\"Tendency to prefer routines\",AMBI_HPI_Impulse_control:\"Tendency to avoid being thoughtless or reckless\",AMBI_HPI_Avoids_trouble:\"Tendency to be agreeable and respect authority\",AMBI_HPI_Science_ability:\"Capacity and preference for analytical and data-informed thinking\",AMBI_HPI_Curiosity:\"Tendency to want to understand how things work\",AMBI_HPI_Thrill_seeking:\"Tendency to prefer exciting and sometimes dangerous activities\",AMBI_HPI_Intellectual_games:\"Preference for complex activities and games of strategy\",AMBI_HPI_Generates_ideas:\"Tendency to come up with novel and innovative ideas\",AMBI_HPI_Culture:\"Tendency to appreciate artistic activities and/or unfamiliar ways of living\",AMBI_HPI_Education:\"Tendency to be knowledgeable in terms of facts and skills\",AMBI_HPI_Math_ability:\"Capacity to be capable of advanced math\",AMBI_HPI_Good_memory:\"Capacity to recall and/or recognize lots of information\",AMBI_HPI_Reading:\"Capacity to read sophisticated texts with high literacy vocabulary terms\",AMBI_HPI_Self_focus:\"Tendency to be introspective and reflective\",AMBI_HPI_Impression_management:\"Tendency to be concerned with the opinions of others, especially about oneself\",AMBI_HPI_Appearance:\"Tendency to be concerned about one’s physical appearance, especially clothes and grooming\"}}};var Ho={AMBI_NEO_Neuroticism:[1.571428571,1.857142857,2,2.142857143,2.25,2.285714286,2.428571429,2.428571429,2.571428571,2.571428571,2.714285714,2.714285714,2.75,2.857142857,2.857142857,2.875,3,3,3,3.125,3.142857143,3.